msgid "Colour the scene and read the legend"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Colour the proxies by type, epoch or a property, and read the legend.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The proxies of the **3D scene** take, at first, the colour of their "
"unit's type, as the Extended Matrix draws it. The **Colours** window "
"gives them other colours, to answer other questions: which units were "
"born in which epoch, which are new in the epoch shown, which share a "
"value of a property, such as the material. The colours are a way of "
"looking: they change the scene, not the units. A set of colours for the "
"values of a property is a **colour scheme**, and it can be kept in a "
"``.emc`` file, the file EM Tools uses for the same purpose, or inside the"
" em.json."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open the study in the **Space** workspace and choose an epoch in the bar "
"of the **3D scene**. The colours apply to the proxies of the epoch shown."
msgstr ""

msgid "Open the Colours window"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Colours** in the bar of the **3D scene**, or choose ``Tools ▸ "
"Colours…``. The window works on the Space it was opened from (from the "
"Tools menu, the active one). Its first line, **Epoch shown:**, names the "
"epoch and the number of its proxies."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Colours window over the Space: the epoch shown, the choice between "
"EM, Epochs and Property, and the colour scheme kept in the file."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The window floats over the page: drag it by its bar and resize it from "
"its corner. **⇱** takes it out into a window of its own, which you can "
"move to another screen, and **⇲** brings it back. The **?** explains each"
" part."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **▾** beside **Colours** in the bar, or a right click on it, opens "
"the quick menu instead: the same three ways of colouring, without the "
"list of values, and **Open the Colours window…**."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose how to colour"
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose one of the three ways at the top of the window."
msgstr ""

msgid "**EM**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each proxy in the colour of its unit's type, as always. This is the "
"starting point."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Epochs**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Two variants. **Epoch of birth** gives every unit the colour of the epoch"
" it was born in, as EM Tools does. **New and survived** looks at the "
"epoch shown: the units born there are in full colour, those born before "
"and still alive are faded. The list gives the epochs with their colours. "
"An epoch's colour belongs to the graph: changing it here changes the "
"colour of the epoch node, as one step of undo."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Property**"
msgstr ""

msgid "One colour for each value of a property. Continue with the steps below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In **Property**, choose the property in the list. Each name is followed "
"by the number of units of the epoch that have it: in Templu Mare, "
"``material``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Read the values found. Each row gives the **Value**, how many **Units** "
"have it, and its colour. Text values start from a qualitative palette, "
"numbers from a scale between their least and their greatest value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To change one colour, click its swatch. To see which units have a value, "
"click the value: the units are selected, and the scene shows them "
"selected."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To recolour all the values at once, choose a **Scale** and press **Apply "
"the scale**. For numbers, **min** and **max** set the ends of the scale. "
"A scale for numbers chosen for text values is allowed, and the window "
"warns that the colours will then follow the alphabetical order."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Look at the two rows at the bottom, which are always there: **has the "
"property, empty value** and **does not have the property**. Each has its "
"colour and its count. Tick **leave as it is** to let the units without "
"the property keep the colour of their type."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every change appears at once in the scene, as a draft. At the foot of the"
" window, **Apply** confirms it, as one step of undo and a line in the "
"**Log**; **Cancel** puts the colours back as they were. Closing the "
"window with changes not applied asks whether to apply them, drop them, or"
" keep editing."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read the legend"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With **Epochs** or **Property**, a legend appears in a corner of the "
"scene: each colour with its epoch or value, and the number of proxies."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A click on a row isolates those proxies, a second click hides them, a "
"third gives them back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**▾** folds the legend into one line, which says how many values it "
"holds; **▸** opens it again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Drag the legend by its head: it goes to the corner of the scene nearest "
"to where you drop it. Its corner resizes it. A long legend shows its "
"first rows, and a last row that says how many more there are opens the "
"rest."
msgstr ""

msgid "**✎** opens the **Colours** window on the same scheme."
msgstr ""

msgid "The legend's place and size are kept for each view, on this computer."
msgstr ""

msgid "Keep a colour scheme"
msgstr ""

msgid "A scheme made for a property can outlive the session in two ways."
msgstr ""

msgid "As a ``.emc`` file"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Save .emc…** writes the scheme as a file, and **Open .emc…** reads one,"
" including those written by EM Tools in Blender. The file keeps the "
"colours of the values and of the two special rows."
msgstr ""

msgid "In the em.json"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The section **In the file**, at the bottom of the window, lists the "
"schemes the study's file holds, the one this graph uses marked **in "
"use**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Keep in the file…** asks for a name and puts the scheme on screen into "
"the file, as the one this graph uses."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Use** colours the graph with a scheme of the file, and writes in the "
"file that the graph uses it; **Stop using** leaves the scheme in the "
"file."
msgstr ""

msgid "**×** takes a scheme out of the file."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Expel to a folder…** writes each scheme as a ``.emc``, then asks "
"whether to take the schemes out of the file: **Take them out** or **Keep "
"them in the file**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Import into the file…** takes one or more ``.emc`` files in, each named"
" after its file."
msgstr ""

msgid "Desktop"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Expel to a folder…** asks for a folder, starting from the em.json's "
"own, and writes the ``.emc`` files there. **Import into the file…** opens"
" the system's dialog, where several files can be picked at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "Web"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Expel to a folder…** downloads one ``.emc`` for each scheme into the "
"browser's downloads. **Import into the file…** opens the browser's file "
"picker."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each of these gestures marks the file as changed, and the message that "
"follows offers to undo it. The schemes are saved with the file, and a "
"study opens with the scheme its graph uses. ``File ▸ Clean the file…`` "
"can take them all out again."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The proxies of the scene are coloured by the question you asked, with a "
"legend to read them. The colours never change the units: the graph keeps "
"its types and its values. The choice of colours stays with the view on "
"this computer, unless you keep the scheme in the file or in a ``.emc``. "
"EM Tools reads both: a scheme in use in the em.json is applied when the "
"file is imported into Blender."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/reference/em-legend`, for the colours and shapes of the Extended "
"Matrix."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`/how-to/files/clean-the-file`, to take the schemes out of the file."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`containers-and-epochs`, for what the scene shows in each epoch."
msgstr ""

